  "label": "Single Cell Perturbation Response Prediction",
  "related": [
    "regulatory-gene-9c82",
    "open-electrophysiological-e8a5",
    "design-principle-hypometabolism-df23"
  ],
  "secondary_categories": [],
  "slug": "ai-topic-c4ec",
  "summary": "New models for unseen genetic and chemical perturbations arrive alongside analyses of prior quality and cross-dataset concordance of drug responses.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is predicting single-cell transcriptional responses to unseen perturbations. Several models inject structure. Graph-guided Flow Matching couples knowledge graphs with latent flow matching for unseen Perturb-seq targets. scBIG++ models the full gene set via gene-module clustering across genetic and chemical perturbations. S1Pert predicts target-specific responses from Welch/equivalence-test labels with a frozen encoder. Disentanglement is a second focus. PertVI separates mitotic phase, cell state, batch and treatment effects. A normalized HSIC regularizer for concept bottleneck generative models targets nonlinear dependence between known and unknown factors in counterfactuals. Evaluation work qualifies these gains. A gene-similarity analysis comparing eight prior sources with Perturb-seq co-function and co-regulation finds that prior agreement tracks target-level predictability. A six-resource benchmark reports limited cross-source concordance of drug-induced responses. Adjacent items cover foundation models (LingoCell, Multicellular Transformer), patient-derived organoid drug-screen data and kinetic metabolic modeling.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-17"
}
